WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.400 --> 00:00:03.200
The A-Z of isms... existentialism.

00:00:04.240 --> 00:00:06.840
There they are,
the quintessential existentialists,

00:00:06.920 --> 00:00:08.880
lounging in a Parisian left-bank café

00:00:08.960 --> 00:00:11.200
discussing the
meaninglessness of life.

00:00:12.160 --> 00:00:15.200
There is some truth behind
every detail of this stereotype

00:00:15.280 --> 00:00:18.200
but in its totality
it’s a misleading caricature.

00:00:19.000 --> 00:00:21.360
It owes much to Jean-Paul Sartre
and Albert Camus,

00:00:21.720 --> 00:00:25.280
both philosophers, writers and famous
public intellectuals in France,

00:00:25.360 --> 00:00:28.480
from the Second World War
until Sartre's death in 1980.

00:00:29.160 --> 00:00:32.560
Sartre argued that the fundamental
principle of existentialism

00:00:32.640 --> 00:00:35.320
is that "existence precedes essence".

00:00:35.840 --> 00:00:38.360
In other words,
we have to create our own lives,

00:00:38.440 --> 00:00:40.560
our own selves, our own values.

00:00:41.240 --> 00:00:42.640
God does not exist.

00:00:42.960 --> 00:00:46.520
We are born without a destiny,
it's all up to us.

00:00:46.600 --> 00:00:48.720
We are therefore
condemned to be free,

00:00:48.800 --> 00:00:51.560
condemned because our freedom
is far from that of the carefree,

00:00:51.640 --> 00:00:53.800
hedonistic consumer
on a shopping spree.

00:00:54.360 --> 00:00:57.160
We have an absolute responsibility
for ourselves,

00:00:57.240 --> 00:00:59.320
which can lead to despair
and anguish,

00:00:59.400 --> 00:01:01.640
even questioning
the point of living -

00:01:01.720 --> 00:01:04.040
the classic "existential crisis"!

00:01:04.720 --> 00:01:07.840
This existential angst
has not gone away -

00:01:08.160 --> 00:01:11.160
it's the human condition,
as real today as ever.

00:01:11.800 --> 00:01:14.000
Political freedom
and material prosperity

00:01:14.080 --> 00:01:17.080
end the struggle for basic
existence and survival,

00:01:17.160 --> 00:01:18.680
but only increase the struggle

00:01:18.760 --> 00:01:20.920
to find genuine meaning
for our existence.

00:01:21.320 --> 00:01:23.920
Why are we here?
How can we make a difference?

00:01:24.000 --> 00:01:25.560
How should we really live?

00:01:26.080 --> 00:01:27.920
Sartre took our freedom as given,

00:01:28.000 --> 00:01:30.200
but his partner and fellow
philosopher Simone de Beauvoir,

00:01:30.280 --> 00:01:32.440
pointed out that
it's not always that simple.

00:01:32.760 --> 00:01:34.880
In her seminal book, The Second Sex,

00:01:34.960 --> 00:01:38.480
she argued that society and
political structures often conspire

00:01:38.560 --> 00:01:39.840
to hold women back.

00:01:39.920 --> 00:01:44.560
Camus argued that, yes we are free,
but the world itself is absurd.

00:01:44.960 --> 00:01:47.000
He retold the classical
myth of Sisyphus,

00:01:47.080 --> 00:01:49.400
condemned forever
to push a rock up a hill,

00:01:49.720 --> 00:01:53.360
only to see it roll back down
for him to start all over again.

00:01:53.760 --> 00:01:58.320
Like Sisyphus, our task is to accept
our own absurd lives cheerfully.

00:01:58.880 --> 00:02:02.360
Wonderful though life can be,
it has no ultimate purpose,

00:02:02.440 --> 00:02:04.480
and we all end as dust.

00:02:04.960 --> 00:02:08.280
However, not all existentialists
believe we live in a godless,

00:02:08.360 --> 00:02:09.680
senseless universe.

00:02:10.000 --> 00:02:11.480
The father of existentialism,

00:02:11.560 --> 00:02:14.240
the 19th Century Dane
Soren Kierkegaard,

00:02:14.320 --> 00:02:15.840
believed in the Christian God,

00:02:15.920 --> 00:02:19.360
but argued reason and evidence
can never prove that he exists.

00:02:19.800 --> 00:02:22.560
We have to take a non-rational
leap of faith.

00:02:22.920 --> 00:02:27.160
At its heart then, existentialism is
not really all about meaninglessness.

00:02:27.560 --> 00:02:29.680
It's about the responsibility
we all have

00:02:29.760 --> 00:02:31.480
to make the right choices in life,

00:02:31.560 --> 00:02:33.680
both for ourselves and for others.

00:02:34.040 --> 00:02:35.960
A heavy responsibility indeed,

00:02:36.040 --> 00:02:39.080
and just as important now
as in post-war Paris.

00:02:42.780 --> 00:02:46.440
Thanks for watching. 
Don’t forget to subscribe! :)

